


Motor control architecture exists out of

There are two frames to work with. The world frame, and the body frame.

The user controls the hardware with real-world units and world frame alt/az coords. These are then internally converted to our body equivalent.



USER CONTROL
	uses realworld values such as degrees per second etc
	user motor functions are:



TRAJECTORY CONTROL

	stores the target position and increments in degrees

	trajectory plans and outputs are in motor encoder counts in float
	
	Works in a position mode or constant velocity mode

	uses TrajcetoryState_t to store trajectory config params
	and trajectory position outputs

MOTOR POSITION CONTROL (PID)

	"reads" the trajectory control output through supervisor 
	functions in encoder counts as int32_t

	uses AxisState_t as control memory and axis velo measurements



